WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.335
(heroic music)

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.254
I'm Nadia Drake
with National Geographic.

00:00:04.254 --> 00:00:06.589
Today, we're at NASA's
Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:08.758
with Chris Evans,
who plays Buzz Lightyear

00:00:08.758 --> 00:00:11.636
in the new Disney and
Pixar movie Lightyear.

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:12.971
Hanging out
with the intrepid

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:14.264
freshly returned from orbit,

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:15.765
astronaut Tom Marshburn.

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:17.142
I have been completely

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:20.103
fascinated
with space travel, all things

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:21.438
NASA since I was a kid.

00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:23.023
So the fact that my
new film, Lightyear,

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:24.733
has let me speak

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:26.317
to actual astronauts

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:27.944
is a real dream come true.

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:29.612
Let's find out what life in space
(heoric music fades out)

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:30.780
is really like.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:31.781
 - [Buzz] Here we go.
(pushes lever forward)

00:00:31.781 --> 00:00:32.949
(spaceship takes off)

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:33.992
- [Mission Control] We have lift off.
(curious music)

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:35.118
And of course, Tom
has actually

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:36.119
been to space.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:37.412
What's it like to look

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:38.955
up into space?

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:40.457
You really fall in love
with the Earth again.

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:43.501
Every continent
has its incredibly beautiful sights.

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:44.836
Do the stars look different?

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:46.504
The stars look gorgeous,

00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:47.505
but they don't twinkle

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:48.757
because there's no atmosphere.

00:00:48.757 --> 00:00:49.841
During the day,

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:51.051
it's just black.

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:52.510
And that is, to me,

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.762
the most riveting thing.

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:55.305
(ship flies through atmosphere)
- [Izzy] I can see stars.

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:56.931
- [Izzy] That is space!
- [Buzz] What is happening right now?

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:58.099
- [Darby] She's afraid of space.

00:00:58.099 --> 00:00:59.934
- [Buzz] What?
- [SOX] She should be, it's horrible!

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:02.562
What was harder than you expected
about being in orbit?

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.147
It's really hard to move

00:01:04.147 --> 00:01:05.648
around with control.

00:01:05.648 --> 00:01:06.524
It's hard to stop.

00:01:06.524 --> 00:01:07.525
Somebody's got to grab you and

00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:08.568
plant you somewhere

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:09.819
and get your feet somewhere.

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:10.862
It amazes me we can get

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:12.280
science done up there

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.573
with everything

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:15.158
floating around all the time.

00:01:15.158 --> 00:01:16.367
In our film,

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:20.121
Lightyear, Buzz has a robot companion,
SOX, this little cat.

00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:21.122
- [SOX] Hello, Buzz.
(Buzz yells)

00:01:21.122 --> 00:01:23.291
I am SOX,
your personal companion robot.

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:24.709
How do you cope with being so alone?

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:26.836
We got each other, but we also have
all the mission controls.

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:28.505
And there are some floating

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:30.298
- [Tom] A.I. robots up there.
- [Nadia] Hmm.

00:01:30.298 --> 00:01:31.800
Like taking pictures where we need them,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:32.801
- [Chris] Wow.
- [Tom] put a light, where we need it.

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:35.428
So I've heard that some astronauts
get space sickness.

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:36.971
- [I.V.A.N] Please record your last words.
(answering machine beep)

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:38.473
Do not vomit inside the vehicle.
(digital tape rewinds)

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:39.849
(recording repeating back)
- [Buzz] Do not vomit inside the vehicle.

00:01:39.849 --> 00:01:41.059
About three out of five

00:01:41.059 --> 00:01:42.435
get some form of nausea.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.812
There's no up, down, left or right.

00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:45.855
So your body feels like it's spinning.

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:47.273
Getting used to
that is kind of tough.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.358
And also, your stomach's floating.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:49.859
Everything
on the inside is floating.

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:51.653
(epic trailer hit)
(pushes button and lever)

00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:52.695
(ship jumps to hyperspeed)
(hyperspeed noises)

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:54.405
- [Mission Control] JPL, this is Mission Control, Houston.

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:55.323
-[Chris] Wow.

00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:57.784
This is Tom, Chris and Nadia.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:00.036
We hear you loud and clear, Tom.

00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:01.121
Great to hear your voice.

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:02.997
Just seems like yesterday
that you were up here with us.

00:02:02.997 --> 00:02:04.624
So, I can only imagine,

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.043
what's some of the hardest parts
of training?

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:09.462
We trained in a giant pool

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.339
called the Neutral Buoyancy Laboratory.

00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:13.216
It enables us to understand

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:14.425
how to move outside.

00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:15.718
Years of training goes

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:18.054
into preparing for the actual flight
itself.

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:19.264
(elevator doors open)
A year of work

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:20.640
for a four minute flight.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.724
- [Buzz] Isn't that something?
- [Díaz] Huh.

00:02:21.724 --> 00:02:22.892
In our film, Buzz

00:02:22.892 --> 00:02:24.102
has to utilize

00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:26.271
lightspeed
to accomplish his missions.

00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:28.106
How fast were you guys moving

00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:29.566
on the journey up there

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:30.859
and how fast you guys moving

00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:32.277
now on ISS?

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:33.945
You know, we essentially start at a relative

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:35.321
speed of zero on the ground.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.739
And then with eight
and a half minutes,

00:02:36.739 --> 00:02:39.492
we're traveling 17,500 miles per hour.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.119
And that's what we're doing right now,

00:02:41.119 --> 00:02:42.620
five miles per second.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.122
- [Kjell] And the other incredible thing
(Chris reacts astonished)

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:45.790
is that when it's time to come
home, we've got to

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.709
shed that speed to get home safely.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:49.419
- [Nadia] I'm curious about
the food on board.

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:51.546
How many Michelin stars
would you give the chefs?

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:52.672
- [Kjell] Our space food lab

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:54.132
does an amazing job, so half of

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:55.758
that food is rehydrated,

00:02:55.758 --> 00:02:58.261
we add water to it.
And then the other half is prepared

00:02:58.261 --> 00:03:00.388
and then sealed
in these kind of metallic envelopes.

00:03:00.388 --> 00:03:02.390
And then we put those
on, essentially a hot plate.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:03.641
(meal squeals, shakes, and poofs)
(microwave bell dings)

00:03:03.641 --> 00:03:04.517
(food slides out and sizzles)

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:05.685
I really miss you guys.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.520
- [Tom] Y'all stay safe,
can't wait to see you.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.605
- [Chris] This was fantastic,

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:10.190
easily the best part of press

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.357
I've ever done and probably

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.067
one of my favorite
conversations of my life.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.068
Roger that.

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:15.862
- [Nadia] Thanks for joining us here at JPL,

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:18.406
and I hope you enjoyed
hanging out with Tom and Chris

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:20.658
and our friends aboard
the International Space Station.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.869
Disney and Pixar's Lightyear.

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:23.870
Rated PG.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.413
Now playing only in theaters.

